it's great to welcome to the program  today Tom wheeler who is the former  chairman of the FCC the Federal  Communications Commission thanks so much  for talking to me today David great to  be with you so I mean to begin with I  think if we want to have a conversation  about what regulation of tech companies  should look like it's important to  understand how we sort of got to where  we are so maybe to start with how have  tech companies relatively successfully  avoided the type of regulation that some  believe should be in place well you know  David it's interesting I I have a new  book out called from Guttenberg to  Google the history of our future and one  of the things that it talks about is how  every time a new technology comes along  it's the innovators that make the rules  and and that's the situation that we're  in now you know it was the same story  whether it was the pioneers who were  pioneering the West or or Rockefeller  and Carnegie because they see the vision  they make the rules and they make the  rules  until such time as those decisions begin  to impact on the rights of others and  the public interest and I think that's  where we are today and so it's time for  us the people through our elected  representatives to take back the  rulemaking and to decide what are the  rules that are in the public interest  not just the interests of the pioneers  yeah I mean of course I agree with you I  don't think it'll come as a shock to my  audience that I do but the issue that  we've had historically particularly in  the last couple of decades is that  because the desires of large  corporations and the wealthy are much  more likely to be made into what becomes  the regulatory environment what makes  the priorities of the House and Senate  and is turned into Bill's what what is  that the average person can actually do  to make sure that what's in the public's  best interest is done I mean look at  look at the net neutrality situation  right millions and millions of comments  the FCC website going down etc etc you  know the story my audience knows the  story and we've got a repeal of net  neutrality as an example so Ken Ken  what's in the public interest really be  successfully lobbied for by the public  well yes and and and the the reality  there is if you don't who will  okay so I mean so you can say all my  goodness I'm just a individual I don't  count well then you might as well turn  it over to the folks who are running the  big companies and and I wouldn't say by  the way that the net neutrality fight is  over no it's not we're in we're in court  right now challenging the the  ridiculousness of the Trump FCC's repeal  of the rules and I think I'm cautiously  optimistic about that and then the House  of Representatives passed a piece of  legislation that that reinstates the  rules so that that's very much in play  and and what we can't fall prey to is a  demand that everything has to happen  quickly this is a this is a long game  there's no question it's a long game and  one of the players that wants to have a  say in that game in terms of the  regulation is the tech companies  themselves and we've seen more and more  companies say oh we are four and we saw  Zuckerberg say it in in testimony under  oath and others I believe regulation is  a good thing and I want to work with our  elected officials to get the the  regulation in place is this something  that we should have any do you believe  that this is a good faith offer that  tech companies are making to participate  in figuring out what the regulation  should be or is it just the the fox  guarding the hen house well I don't  think it's bad faith but I think that it  is a situation of where you stand  depends on where you sit and you know  what I was chairman of the FCC people  would always come  and say oh let me tell you why what is  in my interest is in the public interest  right and and they would define things  very narrowly and what we're seeing  right now are are the tech companies  saying okay you know for instance I'm  for net neutrality but I'm for of that  definition of net neutrality being an  empty bag if you will when it comes to  the reality that many of these platforms  are both social network content  providers and they are also massive ad  brokerage platforms do you believe that  those two sides of many of these  entities need to be separated either via  antitrust or some other means well of  course that's the kind of issue that  Elizabeth Warren and others have been  have been making and I've got a lot of  respect for that I have some concern  that you know you got to love it when  your cell phone someone does not want  you to tell me what your view is on the  anti hey we've solved that problem the  but but you know you got to look at  antitrust is really three prongs yes I  mean first the first prong is is to  break up the companies the difficulty  there is it's a it's a long prong you  know it was it was ten years between the  filing of the suit to break up AT&amp;T and  the actual breakup of a TMT and and so  that's a serious challenge and we also  have a problem that our Supreme Court  right now is hold to the Chicago School  theory that makes it very difficult to  establish arm the second prong of  antitrust enforcement is regulatory and  that's what I tried to do at the FCC and  to take competitive principles and put  them into regulation and the third prong  is this concept of openness that  you and I were just talking about and  and so I think it would be an  interesting concept if rather than just  talking about separating the social  platform from the ad sales activity we  go right to the heart of what is it that  creates the market dominance in both of  those areas and that's their data and if  we're talking about our networks being  open then we probably ought to be  talking about the services that use our  networks and in so doing capture our  personal information then making that  information also in your connectable  just like the networks are so Enzo I  mean just to be clear is that an issue  of privacy regulation is that an issue  of data ownership no I think it's an  issue of two things it goes back to the  old common law concept of the duty to  deal you can't hoard information any  more than the guy who was running the  ferry across the river to tell somebody  that he couldn't ride on the ferry and  then there's also a duty of care which  is okay I've got your information I've  got a fiduciary responsibility to you as  to how I'm going to deal with that  information and and we need to go to at  both of those and if you deal with that  then you're dealing with the core issue  that creates this monopoly power  elsewhere so in the European Union there  is now the general data protection  regulation gdpr and it does a lot of  different things one of the one of the  issues is it goes to the sort of like  the financial risk to Internet companies  for improperly sharing user data it  imposes large fines hypothetically do  you like that framework is there does it  not go far enough maybe does it go too  far no I thought gdpr was a great first  step I'm not sure it can be  a imported wholesale into the United  States because of some of our laws and  traditions but it-it-it established the  principle that there was a  responsibility to the party that was  collecting the data and but let me use  another European example that that kind  of supports they open this principle I  was just talking about and that is that  in the UK the government has required  that the nine largest banks open up or  make open access available with consumer  permission the data that they hold on  consumers so that third parties can  offer innovative new services to those  consumers that the services wouldn't be  available previously because they didn't  have access to the data and the  companies the banks were hoarding that  data and say you can only buy our  services and now they were forced to  open it up and there are two hundred  different service providers that have  sprung up saying hey I've got a  competitive way of a competitive  alternative for consumers to to consider  I think we can bring that kind of  attitude of the United States yeah I  mean that's interesting because that's a  very market ish sort of solution which  is not to say we need to make it so that  the data is not being shared in the  first place or it's not dealing so much  with what some see as the problem of  this data being a marketable commodity  but it's saying let's level the playing  field in letting that data  hypothetically at least be used to  create other things of value so it's I'm  not making a value judgment necessarily  but it's certainly a solution that says  we want more sort of free-market  possibilities rather than a consumer  protection angle is that am i  interpreting that correctly or it goes  back to the concept David of openness  and interconnected interconnection I  mean the the concept behind that  neutrality was open networks now let's  talk about how we have open  data that has been collected through the  use of those networks and when we make  that data available to spur competition  to talk a little bit about the next four  to eight years or you know the next  political cycle so to speak and what it  will happen in terms of both net  neutrality and also tack regulation that  we're talking about what are the  elements of government that are going to  most determine the path forward is it as  simple as if Trump gets reelected not  much will change at the FCC if a  Democrat wins things will change is it  elsewhere we should be looking well I  think you can make that kind of a simple  comparison I mean you you look at you  know what the Trump administration has  done thus far you know it has been to  tear everything down I mean what does  the Trump FCC done they've gone through  and systematically eliminated whatever  they can and but but there's an  interesting thing that's happening as a  result of that David and that is that  the tech companies have perhaps been too  successful they have they have may have  convinced the federal government  particularly the Trump administration to  totally leave them alone and in that  kind of a void they invite others  whether it is states like California  stepping in to pass laws on on privacy  and net neutrality 36 other states that  have stepped up to do something about  net neutrality or countries you  mentioned GDP are a minute ago and other  things that are happening in the EU and  all of a sudden the companies are waking  up and saying oh my golly you know we  don't have anybody establishing a common  set of rules and so we've got 36  different states doing things with net  neutrality golly I'd feel a lot better  if there  only one rule and so the fact that they  did everything possible to kill  meaningful net neutrality at the federal  level has opened them up at the state  level the fact that they have done  everything possible to kill meaningful  privacy regulation I mean we put the  first networked privacy requirements in  place when I was at the FCC and 67 days  after the Trump administration came into  office that the Republican Congress  repealed them and now all at the request  of the companies and now the companies  are seeing California and other states  and countries pop up and with their own  set of rules they're saying oh my golly  I can't have all these different rules  out there I've got to have one set of  rules and so it may be you know there's  a great Oscar Wilde quote he said that  he said that there are two tragedies in  life not getting what you want and  getting it and the reality here is the  tech companies just may have been so  massively successful that they have  forced themselves to embrace federal  regulation that heretofore they had  always been opposing we will of course  continue to follow it it could go a  number of different ways as you are  outlining we've been speaking with  former chairman of the FCC Tom wheeler I  will mention his book which was  published February 26th from Gutenberg  to Google the history of our future such  a pleasure having you on thanks David I  enjoyed it

